{\"response\":15,\"explanation\":\"Using the 601-person sample, those with children report higher extramarital engagement than those without: mean affair frequency 1.67 vs 0.91 and any-affair rate 28.6% vs 15.8%. The distributional difference is statistically significant (Mann–Whitney U p=0.0012) and the any-affair association is significant (chi-square p=0.0015), both in the direction of more engagement among parents. In a logistic regression controlling for gender, age, years married, religiousness, education, occupation, and marriage rating, the children coefficient is positive (OR≈1.49) but not statistically significant (p=0.17), indicating no reliable decrease after adjustment. Overall, the evidence does not support a decrease in affairs with children; if anything, the unadjusted pattern is higher engagement, so the answer is a strong No.\"}
